There is a unique approximable self-map of the one-point domain, namely the identity. Any self-map agrees with the identity on elements, by extensionality and the fact that any two elements are equal.

The function space of the one-point domain with itself has a unique element, the inverse image of the default approximable map under the function-space equivalence. Any element has the same image under that equivalence, by injectivity and equality of any two maps, so it equals the default element.
